\paragraph{CED granularity.}
CED operates effectively at the response level for both RSFT selection and inference-time reranking, but fails at sentence-level granularity: filtering individual evidence sentences by NLI score reduces Evi-F1 by 7.7pp (Recall $-$20pp), as it disrupts co-reference chains across evidence sentences. This suggests that evidence quality is a holistic response property rather than a per-sentence attribute.

\paragraph{Ablation.}
CED and flat-NLI produce comparable performance (Rel-F1 0.4563 vs.\ 0.4561); stricter selection ($k{=}1$) outperforms $k{=}3$ by 1.02 Ign-F1 despite 2.96$\times$ fewer examples (Appendix~\ref{sec:ablation_full}).

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
Our findings suggest three guidelines for practitioners seeking evidence-grounded IE: (1) avoid online RL for multi-objective generation tasks where capability coupling matters; (2) prefer RSFT with strict selection ($k{=}1$) over training data quantity; (3) consider CED reranking at inference for deployment scenarios requiring both high extraction accuracy and evidence quality.

